hat we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$  when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot 1)-(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =-1-1 \\\\ c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299863 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=-2$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot (-2))-2(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =0-8 \\\\ c & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8.\n$$" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=-2$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot (-2))-2(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =0-8 \\\\ c & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299864 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{4y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-5,\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-5) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-4y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=4$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x & =c \\\\ {e}^{0\\cdot 4}-2(4)^{2}-5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =1-32 \\\\ c & =-31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31.\n$$" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{4y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-5,\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-5) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-4y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=4$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x & =c \\\\ {e}^{0\\cdot 4}-2(4)^{2}-5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =1-32 \\\\ c & =-31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299930 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3+4y\\,{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x+4{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ 4{e}^{0\\cdot 1}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3+4y\\,{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x+4{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ 4{e}^{0\\cdot 1}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299931 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we  rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c.\n$$" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we  rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323752 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$" "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186323752 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335688 :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-{x}^{3}.$\n\nLet's check if the equation is exact. To do this, we check to see if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)=6y-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-{x}^{3})=6y-3{x}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the given equation is exact and its solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,f(y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c.\n$$" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-{x}^{3}.$\n\nLet's check if the equation is exact. To do this, we check to see if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)=6y-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-{x}^{3})=6y-3{x}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\n$$\n\nSo, the given equation is exact and its solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,f(y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344992 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).$" "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}(4-x-y).$"] [:db/cas 17592186344992 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}(4-x-y) &=\\operatorname{ln}(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344993 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}.$" "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186344993 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy} &=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344994 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186344994 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345000 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345000 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345001 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\n$$" "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345001 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345002 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\n$$" "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345002 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345003 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$" "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186345003 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186345005 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$" "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186345005 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363804 :question/problem "A research module of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the module’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "A research module of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the module’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $\\mathbf{t}$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363807 :question/problem "A communications beacon of mass $24\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the beacon's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nThe air resistance on the beacon is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the beacon’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the beacon are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "A communications beacon of mass $24\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the beacon's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nThe air resistance on the beacon is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the beacon’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the beacon are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $\\mathbf{t}$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363810 :question/problem "A sampling canister of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the canister is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the canister’s velocity is $9\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the canister are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "A sampling canister of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the canister is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the canister’s velocity is $9\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the canister are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $\\mathbf{t}$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186377695 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377695 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377708 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186377708 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378622 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$" "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378622 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378629 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378629 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378636 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378636 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380041 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$" "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380041 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380048 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$" "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186380048 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-2)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380055 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186380055 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186380242 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186380242 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396571 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''." "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-y) &=\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}(xy)) &=x\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)) &=\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''."] [:db/cas 17592186404482 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation." "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4) &=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y) &=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}) &=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation."] [:db/cas 17592186061544 :answer/value "2\\sin y + C" "2\\operatorname{sin}y+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061545 :answer/value "\\sin y + C" "\\operatorname{sin}y+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061546 :answer/value "-2\\sin y + C" "-2\\operatorname{sin}y+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061547 :answer/value "2\\cos y + C" "2\\operatorname{cos}y+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061548 :answer/value "-2\\cos y + C" "-2\\operatorname{cos}y+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061556 :answer/value "3\\cot z + C" "3\\operatorname{cot}z+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061557 :answer/value "3\\sec z\\tan z + C" "3\\operatorname{sec}z\\operatorname{tan}z+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061558 :answer/value "3\\tan z + C" "3\\operatorname{tan}z+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061559 :answer/value "3\\sec z + C" "3\\operatorname{sec}z+C"] [:db/cas 17592186061560 :answer/v